      "message": "\u003e This looks like you\u0027ve compiled a DTBO and are committing the decompiled tree: OOC, why?\n\nTwo reasons:\n  - Reason 1) To follow host DTBO style: Host DTBOs group relevant nodes together with `/fragment@name`, and decompiled style is necessary for that.\n  - Reason 2) To generate VM DTBO without `__symbols__`: `dtc -@` generates symbols for all labels, and reference from `/host` autogenerates `__symbols__`. My test case includes both DT with/without `__symbols__`, so I added labels manually.\n\n\u003e If that\u0027s the case, do we still need /plugin/ given that we already have all the DTBO-specific DT nodes in the DTS (from the first compilation), which could then be built like any regular DT?\n\n`fdt_apply_overlay()` checks whether DTBO has `/plugin/` because DT base has i.t\n\n\u003e nit: Could you sed -i \u0027s/0x0\\+\\([0-9a-f]\\)/0x\\1/g\u0027 with a bit of manual touch-up to make these files a bit more human readable e.g.\n\n```\n#address-cells \u003d \u003c2\u003e;\n#size-cells \u003d \u003c1\u003e;\nreg \u003d \u003c0x0 0xf00000 0x1000\u003e, \u003c0x0 0xf10000 0x1000\u003e;\nphandle \u003d \u003c4\u003e;\n```\nDone\n\n\u003e and isn\u0027t it possible to use label references like\n\n```\nhost {\n    rng { android,pvmfw,target \u003d \u003c\u0026rng\u003e; };\n};\nfragment@rng {\n    __overlay__ {\n        rng: rng {\n            phandle \u003d \u003c2\u003e;\n? I must admit the double-compilation is making it harder for me to reason about this.\n```\n\nAs I mentioned above, I didn\u0027t pick it to manually generate `__symbols__`",
